<div xmlns:perseus="http://www.perseus.org/meta/perseus.rdfs#" xmlns:dctype="http://purl.org/dc/dcmitype/" xmlns:tufts="http://www.tufts.edu/" xmlns:alph="http://alpheios.net/namespaces/tei" class="alpheios-lex-text_container en">
    <div class="alpheios-lex-entry" lemma-id="n58298" body-key=""><b><span class="alpheios-lex-greek" lang="grc">κόθορνος</span></b> , ὁ, <div class="alpheios-lex-lex_sense alpheios-lex-lex_sense1"><b>A.</b><i>buskin, high boot</i>, <i><span class="en">
				Hdt.
				1.155
			</span></i>, <i><span class="en">
				6.125
			</span></i>, <i><span class="en">
				Ar.
				<u>Lys.</u>
				657
			</span></i>, <i><span class="en">
				Lysipp.
				2
			</span></i>, etc.; worn by tragic actors in heroic characters: hence,
			</div>
        <div class="alpheios-lex-lex_sense alpheios-lex-lex_sense3"><b>2.</b> emblem of Tragedy in
			the person of Dionysus, <i><span class="en">
				Ar.
				<u>Ra.</u>
				47
			</span></i>,<i><span class="en">
				557
			</span></i>. </div>
        <div class="alpheios-lex-lex_sense alpheios-lex-lex_sense3"><b>3.</b> since the
			buskins might be worn on either foot, <span class="alpheios-lex-greek" lang="grc">ὁ
				Κόθορνος</span>, nickname for a <i>trimmer</i> or
				<i>timeserver</i>, such as Theramenes, <i><span class="en">
				X.
				<u>HG</u>
				2.3.31
			</span></i>: prov., 
				“<span class="alpheios-lex-greek" lang="grc">εὐμεταβολώτερος κοθόρνου</span>”
				 <i><span class="en">
					Zen.
					3.93
				</span></i>
			, etc.</div>
